opennyai_summary.facts:
The sample of pasteurized full cream milk was divided into four equal parts then and there by putting into four clean and dry glass bottles and 40 drops of formalin was added in each bottle with the help of clean and dry dropper.
Each bottle containing the sample was separately packed, fastened and sealed according to the provisions of FSS Act and Rules/
Regulations framed thereunder;
(iii) one counterpart of the sample under sealed cover was sent to Food Analyst.
Other two counterparts were deposited with Designated Officer.
As the FBO did not make a request to send the fourth counter part for analysis from NABL Acreedited Laboratory, the fourth sealed counterpart was also deposited with the Designated Officer;
(iv) the Food Analyst vide his report dated 12.01.2015 opined "The sample is substandard because milk solids not fat is less than the prescribed minimum limit of 9.0%;
Food Safety Officer vs Sh.Satish Sharma & Ors.
:: 5 ::
(v) the Designated Officer concerned sent a copy of the Food Analyst report to the respondent's giving them an opportunity to file an appeal against the report of the Food Analyst u/S 46(4) FSS Act for sending one part of the same to the Referral Lab,
if so desired.
Accordingly, one sample was sent to Director, Food Safety and Analytical Quality Control Laboratory, C/o CFTRI, Mysore;
(vi) the Director Referral Food Laboratory, Mysore vide Certificate dated 09.04.2015 reported that sample is substandard as defined u/S 3(1)(zx) FSS Act and does not conform to the standards laid down for full cream milk under the provisions of The Food Safety and Standards(Food Products Standards and Food Additives) Regulations, 2011("FSS(FPS & FA) Regulations" in short) thereof, as  "Milk fat content falls below the minimum standard limit";
(vii) in view of the above, an application/ complaint Food Safety Officer vs Sh.Satish Sharma & Ors.
